ALL THREE ROOT CAUSES CONFIRMED FIRST, then fixed. RC1 and RC3 by reading the source; RC2 empirically — dev.log already contains three occurrences of `[saveJob] clearing the clock trail failed Error: db.$transaction([...]) is not supported on the tenant client`, i.e. the helper threw every time it was actually reached.

DESIGN DECISION — route the edit path through the shared helper, and fix the helper; do NOT reimplement the clearing in the page. This page is already a second copy of saveJob, and a second copy is exactly what produced this bug; a third copy of "what a clock trail is" would also have to re-derive the SETTLED exemption, the log row and the failure reporting. One helper, two call sites (now three).

=== 1. src/lib/job-reschedule.ts — the helper (RC2 + RC3) ===

BEFORE (RC2, atomicity):
  await db.$transaction([
    db.jobWorkSession.deleteMany({ where: { jobId } }),
    db.jobBreak.deleteMany({ where: { jobId } }),
    db.job.update({ ...clear the mirror... }),
  ]);
  await logActivity({ action: "job.reschedule.clock_cleared", ... });

What that actually did: org-db.ts's modelProxy (lines 38-51) makes every model call EAGER — each `db.x.y(...)` is an already-running ordinary Promise by the time the array literal is finished. So all three writes were dispatched, unordered, OUTSIDE any transaction; then `$transaction` (org-db.ts:65-100) rejected the array form and threw. Consequences: (a) no atomicity whatsoever — any subset could land; (b) the throw jumped over the `logActivity` call, so even a clear that physically happened left NO "clock-in data was cleared" row in /admin/logs; (c) the caller's `.catch(console.error)` swallowed it; (d) the three promises were left floating unhandled.

AFTER:
  try {
    await db.$transaction(async (tx) => {
      await tx.jobWorkSession.deleteMany({ where: { jobId } });
      await tx.jobBreak.deleteMany({ where: { jobId } });
      if (assignments.length > 0) {
        await tx.jobAssignment.updateMany({
          where: { id: { in: assignments.map((a) => a.id) } },
          data: { status: "ASSIGNED", onMyWayAt: null, clockInTime: null, clockOutTime: null },
        });
      }
      await tx.job.update({ where: { id: jobId }, data: { ...unchanged mirror clear... } });
    });
  } catch (e) {
    console.error(`[job-reschedule] clearing the clock trail for job ${jobId} failed`, e);
    await logActivity({ category: "ADMIN", action: "job.reschedule.clock_clear_failed",
      status: "FAILED", targetType: "Job", targetId: jobId,
      message: `Rescheduled ${job.clientName}'s job, but its clock-in data could NOT be cleared — the job may still show as clocked in at its new date. Check Time Tracking.`,
      error: e instanceof Error ? e.message : String(e) });
    throw e;
  }

RC3 (the JobAssignment row) — new, before the transaction:
  const assignments = job.assignments.filter(
    (a) => a.status !== "CANCELLED" &&
      (a.status !== "ASSIGNED" || a.onMyWayAt != null || a.clockInTime != null || a.clockOutTime != null));
CANCELLED rows are deliberately excluded: that cleaner was taken off the job and resetting them to ASSIGNED would put them back on it. `assignments.length > 0` was added to `hasTrail`, so an assignment-only trail (job mirror already clean) is still cleared. `ClearedClockTrail` gained `assignments: number`, and the success log row now ends "...and 1 cleaner assignment was reset to Assigned."

SETTLED exemption untouched: the `if (!job || SETTLED.includes(job.status)) return null` early return still precedes everything, so COMPLETED/PAID jobs never reach the transaction.

Loudness: a failure is now recorded in BOTH surfaces the defect report named as missing — an ActivityLog row with status FAILED (visible in /admin/logs) and a console.error line in dev.log — and rethrown so the caller can react.

=== 2. src/app/admin/jobs/new/page.tsx — the reported path (RC1) ===

+ import { clearClockTrailForReschedule } from "@/lib/job-reschedule";

The `moneyJob` pre-update fetch gained `startTime: true` (the only way this branch can tell a reschedule from a save that left the date alone — it must be the value BEFORE db.job.update).

Immediately after `await db.job.update(...)` in the edit branch, and BEFORE `syncJobAssignments` (which throws on failure — the clear must not be skipped because an unrelated team change broke):

  if (startDate && startTime && jobData.startTime && moneyJob?.startTime) {
    if (moneyJob.startTime.getTime() !== jobData.startTime.getTime()) {
      await clearClockTrailForReschedule(editingJobId).catch((e) =>
        console.error("[admin/jobs/new] clearing the clock trail failed", e));
    }
  }

The `startDate && startTime` guard mirrors the shared actions/saveJob: with no date fields posted, `jobData.startTime` falls back to `new Date()`, which would read as a move on every save and end a live shift because somebody edited a note. The `.catch` keeps the redirect intact (the job row already moved; an exception page would tell the admin nothing saved) — the failure is not silent because the helper itself already wrote the FAILED /admin/logs row and the dev.log line.

Duplication was not touched. updateJobDates.ts and actions/saveJob.ts were not touched — they call the same helper and simply stop failing.

DEFECT CONFIRMED. The cleaner door had a single catch-all: `if (from === "cleaner") { if (!isCleanerRole(role)) bounce("/cleanos/login?error=not_cleaner") }`. Every non-EMPLOYEE — admin, CLIENT, applicant — got the one value `not_cleaner`, whose message is "That account isn't a cleaner account. If you're an admin, use the staff sign-in." A customer following that lands on /sign-in and is bounced a second time (to /login?error=use_customer_login). Exactly the sharing the file's own comment says `staff_account` used to do.

FIX 1 — src/app/api/post-signin/route.ts, cleaner-door block (new lines 142-149). Split the CLIENT case out ahead of the generic one, with a new distinct value:

BEFORE
  if (from === "cleaner") {
    if (!isCleanerRole(role)) {
      return bounce(`/cleanos/login?error=not_cleaner`, "not a cleaner account");
    }

AFTER
  if (from === "cleaner") {
    if (isClientRole(role)) {
      // NOT `not_cleaner`: that message carries the *admin* remedy ("use the
      // staff sign-in"), and a customer who follows it is bounced a second
      // time. A customer standing at the cleaner door needs the customer
      // login, so the case gets its own value — same reasoning as
      // staff_account/use_customer_login and not_cleaner/use_cleaner_login.
      return bounce(`/cleanos/login?error=customer_account`, "customer should use the portal login");
    }
    if (!isCleanerRole(role)) {
      return bounce(`/cleanos/login?error=not_cleaner`, "not a cleaner account");
    }

Value named `customer_account` to mirror `staff_account` — both name the ACCOUNT type of whoever arrived at the wrong door, and both keep the person on the door they landed on where a link to the right door already sits. isClientRole was already imported; no new imports.

FIX 2 — src/app/(auth)/cleanos/login/page.tsx (lines 29-45). Added the matching branch between the two existing ones and rewrote the block comment from "two cases" to three:

    errorParam === "not_cleaner"
      ? "That account isn't a cleaner account. If you're an admin, use the staff sign-in."
      : errorParam === "customer_account"
        ? "That's a customer account — use the customer login link below."
        : errorParam === "use_cleaner_login"
          ? "That's a cleaner account — this is the right place. Sign in below to see your jobs."
          : null;

"use the customer login link below" points at the footer link that already exists on that form ("Are you a customer? Go to the customer login →", href /login?stay=1) — the one thing the reporter found that led anywhere useful.

FULL TABLE — every ?error= emitted by post-signin, verified by rendering each one in a browser:

| ?error= | route.ts | Direction | Shown on | Message on screen |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| staff_account | :135 | non-CLIENT turned away at the CUSTOMER door (?from=portal) | /login | That's a staff account — use the crew sign-in link below. |
| customer_account (NEW) | :148 | CLIENT turned away at the CLEANER door (?from=cleaner) | /cleanos/login | That's a customer account — use the customer login link below. |
| not_cleaner | :151 | other non-EMPLOYEE turned away at the CLEANER door | /cleanos/login | That account isn't a cleaner account. If you're an admin, use the staff sign-in. |
| not_applicant | :159 | non-APPLICANT turned away at the APPLICANT door (?from=applicant) | /applicant-login | That account isn't an applicant portal account. |
| use_cleaner_login | :168 | EMPLOYEE turned away at the STAFF door (/sign-in) | /cleanos/login | That's a cleaner account — this is the right place. Sign in below to see your jobs. |
| use_customer_login | :177 | CLIENT turned away at the STAFF door | /login | That's a customer account — this is the right place. Sign in below to see your bookings. |
| use_applicant_login | :180 | APPLICANT turned away at the STAFF door | /applicant-login | That's an applicant account — this is the right place. Sign in below to track your application. |
| no_access | :182 | role with no staff access at the STAFF door (fallback) | /sign-in | This account doesn't have staff access. If you're a cleaner, use the cleaner login. |

All four doors now branch on their own values and no message sends anyone back into a second bounce.

All changes are in src/lib/email.ts, in deliver()'s plumbing. No schema change, no new model, no new UI.

1) getResend() — BEFORE:
   function getResend() {
     if (!process.env.RESEND_API_KEY) {
       console.warn("[email] RESEND_API_KEY is not set — emails will be skipped");
       return null;
     }
     return new Resend(process.env.RESEND_API_KEY);
   }
   AFTER (comment explains WHY the warn moved to the caller):
   function getResend() {
     // No warning here any more. It used to print "[email] RESEND_API_KEY is not
     // set" and nothing else, which told an operator that SOME mail had been
     // skipped but never which one, to whom, or about what. Only the caller has
     // that context, so the caller logs it — see recordSkippedEmail().
     if (!process.env.RESEND_API_KEY) return null;
     return new Resend(process.env.RESEND_API_KEY);
   }

2) deliver()'s missing-key branch — BEFORE:
   const resend = getResend();
   if (!resend) {
     if (opts.logId) {
       await db.emailLog.update({
         where: { id: opts.logId },
         data: { status: "FAILED", error: "RESEND_API_KEY not configured" },
       }).catch(() => {});
     }
     return { ok: false, error: "RESEND_API_KEY not configured" };
   }
   AFTER:
   const resend = getResend();
   if (!resend) {
     await recordSkippedEmail(opts, "RESEND_API_KEY not configured");
     return { ok: false, error: "RESEND_API_KEY not configured" };
   }

3) Two new module-private helpers under the existing "Send + log helper" banner, both fully commented in the file's WHY style:

   maskAddress(addr) — returns e.g. "zz***@cleano.test". Doc comment: the full address belongs in the EmailLog row (tenant-scoped, behind RLS); stdout is neither, because dev.log and the hosting log drain are read by people with no business seeing a customer's email address.

   recordSkippedEmail(opts, reason) — doc comment records that the in-app feed already writes its row unconditionally (admin-notifications.ts) so the EVENT is checkable, while the EMAIL leg had no equivalent; and that cost is one row on a path that makes no network call, so a skip stays strictly cheaper than the send it stands in for and cannot amplify writes past what a configured RESEND_API_KEY already costs. Body:
     - console.warn(`[email] skipped (${reason}) — key=${key ?? "ungated"} to=${maskAddress(opts.to)}` + (opts.jobId ? ` job=${opts.jobId}` : ""))
       with an inline comment that the subject is deliberately left out because most of these carry the customer's name.
     - if opts.logId → stamp that existing row (status FAILED, error reason), unchanged behaviour, no second row.
     - else → db.emailLog.create({ data: { kind: "OTHER", recipient: opts.to, subject: opts.subject, status: "FAILED", error: reason, notificationKey: key, jobId: opts.jobId ?? null } }).catch(() => {})
       Inline comment notes OTHER + notificationKey is exactly how /admin/logs titles a catalog-driven mail (emailTitle() in src/lib/log-labels.ts), since the EmailKind enum only names the transactional ones. FAILED is the only honest value: EmailStatus is PENDING|SENT|FAILED — there is no SKIPPED — and FAILED + an error string is already the idiom the toggle-off branch two lines above uses. The .catch(() => {}) carries a comment that this is bookkeeping about a send that already did not happen and must never be the reason a caller fails.

   A further doc-comment paragraph states it is deliberately NOT used for the Settings → Notifications skip: that is a standing admin choice, so a row per suppressed email would pile up forever and tell the admin only what they configured themselves. That branch is left byte-for-byte unchanged.

4) deliver() opts gained an optional `jobId?: string` with a doc comment ("The job this mail is about. Used only for the EmailLog row, so a send that was skipped is still traceable to a record in /admin/logs instead of being one more anonymous failure. Optional: senders with no job simply leave it off and stay identifiable by key + recipient.").

5) Three call sites now pass it: sendAdminShiftDropped's deliver(), and both legs of sendAdminJobIssue (admin.job.issue_reported and admin.job.issue_urgent) — the two senders named in the defect report. Every other deliver() call site is untouched.

Net behaviour: nothing is sent that was not sent before, nothing is sent that was suppressed before. The only additions are one console line and one EmailLog row per email that was already being silently dropped.
